00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:10,320
Not going to get into that.

88
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:15,900
But in Federalist 10, James Madison explained that we are a large country, we got a lot

89
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:22,960
of disparate states, and to get anything done at all, people are going to have to work together.

90
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:23,960
And he was right.

91
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:28,040
We got parties almost immediately, and we still got them now.

92
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:31,640
But, to your question, Hannah, what are parties?

93
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:33,800
Well, so let's take a step back.

94
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,100
What are parties, right?

95
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:39,260
Because the DSA is often thought of as a political party.

96
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:41,400
And so for political scientists,

97
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:44,260
we can think about parties as organizations

98
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:46,660
that operate on like three different dimensions.

99
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:48,540
Three dimensions.

100
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:50,180
You know I love a list, Hannah.

101
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:51,140
And a party.

102
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:54,500
Taylor wrote, do you love parties?

103
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:55,460
And I was like, I used to.

104
00:05:55,460 --> 00:05:56,980
I don't know if you do, actually.

105
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:00,020
I used to be like the partiest party animal in the world.

106
00:06:00,020 --> 00:06:00,900
What happened?

107
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,140
People don't have parties anymore.

108
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:03,620
That's what happened.

109
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:08,460
Dimension number one, parties as they operate in government.

110
00:06:08,460 --> 00:06:11,340
So those are the coalitions of office holders

111
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:15,020
who sort of pre-commit to work together

112
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,100
to advance a shared agenda.

113
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:20,540
These are people in office, Hannah, legislators

114
00:06:20,540 --> 00:06:23,020
and their staff who govern.

115
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:25,140
They write bills, they vote on bills

116
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:28,180
and most of the time they support each other's work.

117
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:29,660
And also, a lot of the time,

118
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:32,300
oppose the work coming from the other side.

119
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:33,940
Like when you watch the State of the Union

120
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:36,220
and everyone is sitting in the House chamber

121
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:38,420
and they're either on the left or the right side

122
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:40,700
split into teams as it were,

123
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:42,940
I always sort of thought of it as the groom side

124
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:44,140
or the bride side.

125
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:45,340
It's a lot like that.

126
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:48,700
And each of those sides have their own agenda,

127
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:50,620
which they share with the rest of their party.

128
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:51,780
The party platform, right?

129
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:52,620
Exactly.

130
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:55,820
And the party platform has myriad planks in it.

131
00:06:55,820 --> 00:06:59,020
What are we as Republicans or we as Democrats

132
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:01,700
trying to achieve right now as a bloc?

133
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:06,700
Okay, party dimension number two, parties as organizations.

134
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:12,220
A network of candidates, interest groups, office holders,

135
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:16,140
who channel resources and information

136
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:19,260
and campaign assistance to each other.

137
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:22,700
And you could think of it almost like a movement.

138
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:24,220
Resources and assistance.

139
00:07:24,220 --> 00:07:26,620
So first and foremost, money, right?

140
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:27,460
Lots of money.

141
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:28,780
And then there's so much more, right?

142
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:31,620
There are campaign workers, volunteers.

143
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:33,260
You've got people knocking on doors,

144
00:07:33,260 --> 00:07:35,620
you've got pollsters and think tanks

145
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:37,460
and lawyers and fundraisers,

146
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:39,080
and then you've got the various committees

147
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:41,880
that actually draft the platforms.

148
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:45,520
The two main parties are big and intricate organizations

149
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:49,820
and a ton of people work within those organizations.

150
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:53,480
Yes, finally, the third dimension parties

151
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:56,520
in the electorate, which is you and me

152
00:07:56,520 --> 00:07:58,060
and everyone out there listening.

153
00:07:58,060 --> 00:08:00,540
We the people who vote.

154
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:03,260
How do we interact with the party?

155
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:05,760
We're often thinking about the information

156
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:08,500
that the party brand, the name that's associated

157
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:11,140
with the organization, what that conveys to voters.

158
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:12,620
And so we typically think that like one thing

159
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:15,700
that parties do in the electorate is that for most voters,

160
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:18,620
most people have, you know, we talk about politics

161
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:20,860
as a sideshow to life, right?

162
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:22,820
Most of us are not spending all of our time

163
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:23,740
thinking about politics.

164
00:08:23,740 --> 00:08:27,620
And so what party brands or names do is they convey

165
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:31,700
information about candidates or existing office holders,

166
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:34,580
policy commitments or values or ideas

167
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:37,500
or our way to predict how they're likely to govern.

168
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,660
And so to illustrate this third dimension, Hannah,

169
00:08:40,660 --> 00:08:43,620
let us say your uncle Vernon stands up

170
00:08:43,620 --> 00:08:45,820
and calls himself a libertarian

171
00:08:45,820 --> 00:08:47,540
at the McCarthy family Christmas.

172
00:08:48,980 --> 00:08:51,380
Among all those JFK Irish candidates,

173
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:55,620
I do not actually have an uncle Vernon everyone,

174
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:57,020
but I understand where you're going, Nick.

175
00:08:57,020 --> 00:09:00,400
Right, so what Vern means, does he go by Vern?

176
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:01,960
He does.

177
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:06,180
So what Vern means, isn't that he's literally a politician

178
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:08,700
who's running for office as a libertarian,

179
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:11,620
nor does it mean he got a job as the communications director

180
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:13,540
for the local chapter of the Libertarian Party.

181
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:16,860
Right, ol' Vern means I like libertarian ideas

182
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:19,300
and I intend to vote that way.

183
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:21,940
Exactly, thank you for sharing your opinion, Vern.

184
00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:26,620
So taken in the aggregate, these three party dimensions,

185
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:29,380
you have basically a party brand.

186
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:32,620
And for a lot of folks, even the independence out there,

187
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:36,460
that brand is a huge part of how they vote.

188
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:38,700
As evidenced by people being a bit confused

189
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:40,300
as to what to do in the voting booth

190
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:43,060
when they do not see a capital D or a capital R

191
00:09:43,060 --> 00:09:45,580
or a capital I next to someone's name

192
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:49,180
who is running for say registrar of deeds.

193
00:09:49,180 --> 00:09:51,660
Party affiliation is, I think,

194
00:09:51,660 --> 00:09:53,820
one of the most important factors

195
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:56,100
for the majority of American voters.

196
00:09:56,100 --> 00:09:56,940
Absolutely.

197
00:09:56,940 --> 00:10:01,940
All right, so that's parties.

198
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:05,000
All wrapped up with pretty pink ribbons on it.

199
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,860
Let's talk about parties within parties.

200
00:10:07,860 --> 00:10:09,580
Factions within factions.

201
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:11,340
Wheels within wheels.

202
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:15,300
Zayana, occasionally we see the formation of factions,

203
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:19,440
caucuses and coalitions inside political parties.

204
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:22,780
So take for example, a recent wave of candidates

205
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:25,660
who may be listed on the ballot as Democrats,

206
00:10:25,660 --> 00:10:28,720
But they distinguish themselves from their peers

207
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:31,580
by referring to themselves as democratic socialists.

208
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:34,260
And in some cases, who have been endorsed

209
00:10:34,260 --> 00:10:36,180
by a different organization called

210
00:10:36,180 --> 00:10:39,460
the Democratic Socialists of America, the DSA.

211
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:41,560
The democratic socialists seem to be doing

212
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:43,540
considerably better than they used to be,

213
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:45,460
and they have the chance to knock off, in fact,

214
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:47,380
democratic incumbents in Congress.

215
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:48,860
And part of the reason for that,

216
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:50,580
why we're seeing these democratic socialists,

