00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:38,860
about policy ideas and things like that.

236
00:11:38,860 --> 00:11:41,220
And then I think we have a fair amount of evidence

237
00:11:41,220 --> 00:11:44,180
that identifying as a democratic socialist

238
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:46,060
conveys information to voters

239
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:48,700
about how they're different from other candidates

240
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:50,980
who are running with other party brands.

241
00:11:50,980 --> 00:11:54,440
And so they're telling voters by identifying

242
00:11:54,440 --> 00:11:55,880
as a member of the DSA,

243
00:11:55,880 --> 00:11:57,320
I'm gonna govern differently.

244
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:01,080
There are different policy priorities that I have.

245
00:12:01,080 --> 00:12:02,800
And more on those policies later, Hannah,

246
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:04,720
but first I wanna talk about one thing

247
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:07,520
that this influx of DSA candidates has in common

248
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:10,040
with previous examples of inter-party factions.

249
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:12,600
And that is that it's happening at a time

250
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:15,440
of dissatisfaction with the status quo.

251
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:19,080
The upshot for those people who are dissatisfied

252
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:20,720
is that when these happen,

253
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:25,520
they tend to be a bellwether for significant change.

254
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:29,200
Interprety organizations are usually really core players

255
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:32,080
in major policy debates across congressional history,

256
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:33,980
and that's certainly been true of the 20th century.

257
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:38,160
So if we think about big changes in fights within Congress

258
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:41,200
in the progressive era over tariff policy,

259
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:42,780
railroad regulation.

260
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:44,920
This was the internal feud over tariffs

261
00:12:44,920 --> 00:12:48,020
in the McKinley administration that 10 years later

262
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:50,600
led Teddy Roosevelt to completely break off

263
00:12:50,600 --> 00:12:52,000
from the Republicans at the time

264
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,360
and formed the short-lived anti-corruption

265
00:12:54,360 --> 00:12:56,960
tax the wealthy bull moose party,

266
00:12:56,960 --> 00:13:01,520
which started as a sort of rebellion inside the party.

267
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:04,360
I believe again that the American people are as a whole

268
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:08,120
capable of self-control and of learning by their mistake.

269
00:13:08,120 --> 00:13:10,320
Progressive Republicans and populist Democrats

270
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:13,560
were key foot soldiers in those battles,

271
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:16,520
within their parties and then often working together.

272
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:20,160
In the mid-century, we think about the civil rights era,

273
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:25,520
Fights over passing civil rights legislation took place primarily between conservative

274
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:31,280
southern Democrats and liberal Democrats over the direction of the Democratic Party and

275
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:35,920
used their respective organizations to either promote civil rights or try and delay civil

276
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:40,320
rights through Congress's committee system or on the floor.

277
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:45,440
And Nick, you and I know what happened after that intraparty fight. The country saw what

278
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:52,240
political scientists call a realignment. The Democrats and the Republicans dramatically

279
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:57,680
shifted their ideologies. And for a much more recent example of an intraparty taking Shea

280
00:13:57,680 --> 00:14:04,240
Panna, let's take a little trip back in time to the 2008 presidential election.

