508
00:26:15,760 --> 00:26:18,800
This is not where I want the Democratic Party to go.

509
00:26:18,800 --> 00:26:25,880
I am tired, tired, tired of a set of people on the very far left in the Democratic Party,

510
00:26:25,880 --> 00:26:30,440
my party, telling us that in order for us to be progressive, we now have to believe

511
00:26:30,440 --> 00:26:33,680
ideas and support ideas that are completely nuts.

512
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:37,660
Prominent Democrat voices pushing back on the growing influence of the Democratic Socialist

513
00:26:37,660 --> 00:26:42,040
candidates, they say are ultimately going to hurt the Democrat Party.

514
00:26:42,040 --> 00:26:49,160
And so I think in that way, the work of the DSA fits well within this sort of battle for

515
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:50,560
the soul of the party.

516
00:26:50,560 --> 00:26:55,760
What's interesting, I think, about the DSA is that a lot of the candidates have, I think,

517
00:26:55,760 --> 00:27:03,240
a more oppositional relationship to the main party that they are trying to change.

518
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:08,440
I think, given our current politics, that makes sense.

519
00:27:08,440 --> 00:27:12,020
If you think about what we know about public opinion, Americans are disenchanted by the

520
00:27:12,020 --> 00:27:13,020
both parties?

521
00:27:13,020 --> 00:27:17,380
Now you know I like numbers backing things up, Nick.

522
00:27:17,380 --> 00:27:19,700
How disenchanted do we mean here?

523
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:25,240
Well there was a survey in April of 2026 from the Pew Research Center which asked Americans

524
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:30,760
if they had a favorable or unfavorable opinion of the Republican and Democratic parties.

525
00:27:30,760 --> 00:27:32,740
And the numbers were nearly identical.

526
00:27:32,740 --> 00:27:39,340
58% of people polled had an unfavorable view of the Republican party and 59% of the Democratic

527
00:27:39,340 --> 00:27:40,340
party.

528
00:27:40,340 --> 00:27:45,140
Now, you might want to just chalk this up to hyper partisanship, but pair that with

529
00:27:45,140 --> 00:27:49,700
the fact that the public is pretty evenly split by those who identify as Democrats or

530
00:27:49,700 --> 00:27:52,360
Republicans, and the math bears out.

531
00:27:52,360 --> 00:27:56,860
There are a lot of people out there unhappy with both parties.

532
00:27:56,860 --> 00:28:00,380
And so to gain political power, you have to resonate with voters.

533
00:28:00,380 --> 00:28:05,880
And so having more oppositional orientation to the party that you would like to change

534
00:28:05,880 --> 00:28:12,280
makes sense and in other areas where parties were more popular, having an oppositional approach

535
00:28:12,280 --> 00:28:14,040
to those parties would have been more costly.

536
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:20,920
Okay, but if you think there's some tension in the Democratic Party, outside of it, the

537
00:28:20,920 --> 00:28:24,560
DSA is used as a sort of catch-all boogeyman.

538
00:28:24,560 --> 00:28:30,120
Hassan Piker is a top surrogate for DSA, so we'll be asking our guests about his statements

539
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:31,120
and much more.

540
00:28:31,120 --> 00:28:34,520
We have Fidel Castro's daughter on as well to respond to this news.

541
00:28:34,520 --> 00:28:37,160
She knows a thing or two about what socialism actually does.

542
00:28:37,160 --> 00:28:40,600
She may know a few things about what this may do in the history that we've seen in.

543
00:28:40,600 --> 00:28:47,720
We do see candidates, office holders of the opposing party, trying and characterize their

544
00:28:47,720 --> 00:28:54,200
own rivals by associating them with what they perceive to be the most unpopular elements

545
00:28:54,200 --> 00:28:55,680
of the opposition.

546
00:28:55,680 --> 00:29:01,080
This is consistently something that smart, savvy candidates do.

547
00:29:01,080 --> 00:29:03,120
You paint with broad brushstrokes.

548
00:29:03,120 --> 00:29:10,120
You lump people in, you conflate policy differences, and you say they're all for X, Y, or Z.

549
00:29:10,120 --> 00:29:12,920
Honestly, Hannah, this is a really common tactic.

550
00:29:12,920 --> 00:29:17,980
You see members of the Democratic Party saying that any Republican is pro-Trump or MAGA regardless

551
00:29:17,980 --> 00:29:19,480
of their voting record.

552
00:29:19,480 --> 00:29:20,800
That's not a new tactic.

553
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:28,700
I think what is so animating for people about Democratic socialists is that the public opinion

554
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:35,100
data we have about the public's perception of what socialism is or what or who socialists

555
00:29:35,100 --> 00:29:41,500
are is not particularly favorable. And so there are certainly folks who are concerned that

556
00:29:41,500 --> 00:29:46,220
identifying as democratic socialist or lumping democratic socialists in with Democrats or

557
00:29:46,220 --> 00:29:52,220
lumping Democrats in with democratic socialists runs the risk of alienating voters and that

558
00:29:52,220 --> 00:29:57,260
whatever branding value you're getting from identifying as a democratic socialist creates

559
00:29:57,260 --> 00:30:03,460
a real risk either to you or to other members of the party with which you are closely affiliated.

560
00:30:03,460 --> 00:30:09,100
And yes, Republicans have seized on that word as well as other words to campaign against

561
00:30:09,100 --> 00:30:10,100
DSA members.

562
00:30:10,100 --> 00:30:11,740
We are the greatest nation in the history world.

563
00:30:11,740 --> 00:30:15,980
We are fighting right now in Congress over whether we're going to maintain our status

564
00:30:15,980 --> 00:30:20,060
as a constitutional republic or we're going to trade that in, dismantle the foundations

565
00:30:20,060 --> 00:30:23,700
and go down this dark road of death to communism.

566
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:29,060
But then you got to ask, is that branding effective?

567
00:30:29,060 --> 00:30:33,800
I think an open question is how much attention voters are actually paying to all of that

568
00:30:33,800 --> 00:30:38,540
when they're getting into the voting booth and making their choices.

569
00:30:38,540 --> 00:30:43,460
And if we think that voters are often most attentive to their pocketbook and their own

570
00:30:43,460 --> 00:30:50,100
financial comfort and the cost of living, then this may just be a big sideshow to the

571
00:30:50,100 --> 00:30:53,940
bigger question that's going to animate how voters evaluate candidates, which is, you

572
00:30:53,940 --> 00:30:59,500
know, is this person going to make me and my family better off or not? And so, you know,

573
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:06,340
you might say that a lot of the current consternation about democratic socialists is an attempt

574
00:31:06,340 --> 00:31:12,460
to distract from the bigger liability for the party in power right now. Republicans,

575
00:31:12,460 --> 00:31:18,220
which is that, you know, whether through their own policies or, you know, broader secular

576
00:31:18,220 --> 00:31:21,900
trends, folks are very concerned about the cost of living, inflation is high.

577
00:31:21,900 --> 00:31:24,700
And so you need to talk about something and you don't want to talk about the

578
00:31:24,700 --> 00:31:25,180
economy.

579
00:31:25,180 --> 00:31:26,860
And so you talk about socialists instead.

580
00:31:27,220 --> 00:31:31,340
And Nick, like Ruth said, like you said, this kind of painting with the broad

581
00:31:31,340 --> 00:31:33,780
negative brush is par for the course.

582
00:31:34,620 --> 00:31:41,340
But what I find interesting is that you also see Democrats this year directly

583
00:31:41,340 --> 00:31:46,900
attacking members of the DSA using that same negative brush for their own

584
00:31:46,900 --> 00:31:53,860
in triparty. Some Democrats also attack the DSA over the socialism thing, which makes me wonder

585
00:31:54,500 --> 00:32:00,180
what happens when you campaign as a radical. You want to make all of these big changes and then

586
00:32:00,180 --> 00:32:06,100
you get elected and you find out that politics is about a lot of negotiation and compromise.

587
00:32:06,740 --> 00:32:14,100
You know, just like the Tea Party had to work on some level with the rest of the GOP, if a wave

588
00:32:14,100 --> 00:32:20,500
of DSA candidates are actually elected, they're going to have to operate inside the Democratic

589
00:32:20,500 --> 00:32:27,340
Party. I mean, if you're an intraparty winner, are your hopes and dreams just dashed as soon

590
00:32:27,340 --> 00:32:28,340
as you take office?

591
00:32:28,340 --> 00:32:33,980
Yeah, this is something I asked Ruth about. How do you walk that balance of working inside

592
00:32:33,980 --> 00:32:38,340
of a system that you're maybe trying to change?

593
00:32:38,340 --> 00:32:44,560
I mean, one thing that I do think is interesting about folks like Mandani or really any person

594
00:32:44,560 --> 00:32:50,580
who's running sort of outside the box is they're going to have to govern with people who are

595
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:52,420
operating inside the box.

596
00:32:52,420 --> 00:32:58,580
And so you create a potentially challenging situation where you promised voters or maybe

597
00:32:58,580 --> 00:33:01,340
you haven't promised them, but you sort of conveyed to voters that you're going to do

598
00:33:01,340 --> 00:33:02,340
things differently.

599
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:05,660
And so you're committed to doing that, but you have to work with a bunch of people who

600
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:07,620
are not committed to doing things differently.

601
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:11,860
And so invariably, you're going to make compromises and you're not going to be able to do all

602
00:33:11,860 --> 00:33:12,860
the things that you wanted.

603
00:33:12,860 --> 00:33:15,020
Do you have an example of this?

604
00:33:15,020 --> 00:33:16,180
I do.

605
00:33:16,180 --> 00:33:22,900
So very recently in New York City, which is facing a very tight budget, I have to say,

606
00:33:22,900 --> 00:33:28,500
recently elected mayor Zoran Mamdani, who supported teachers and unions in his campaign,

607
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:35,500
sued to block a bill that would give $10,000 to teacher aides, an act of his that directly

608
00:33:35,500 --> 00:33:39,860
opposes the city council and the very powerful teachers union.

609
00:33:39,860 --> 00:33:44,260
A spokesperson for the mayor said, quote, we will not allow the political process to

610
00:33:44,260 --> 00:33:47,100
replace the collective bargaining table.

611
00:33:47,100 --> 00:33:52,180
And so then the challenge becomes conveying to voters that you are still serious and committed

612
00:33:52,180 --> 00:33:56,660
to the things you said you were and that the whole system isn't broken just because you've

613
00:33:56,660 --> 00:34:02,460
had to do things that aren't exactly what you had promised because that's any kind of

614
00:34:02,460 --> 00:34:05,940
collective decision-making system requires compromise.

615
00:34:05,940 --> 00:34:09,260
And I think, you know, this is important for students

616
00:34:09,260 --> 00:34:12,340
to appreciate that, you know,

617
00:34:12,340 --> 00:34:14,140
you're not always gonna get everything you want.

618
00:34:14,140 --> 00:34:15,540
And I think in our current moment

619
00:34:15,540 --> 00:34:16,820
where it feels like some people

620
00:34:16,820 --> 00:34:18,900
are getting exactly what they want,

621
00:34:18,900 --> 00:34:20,360
it's hard to explain, you know,

622
00:34:20,360 --> 00:34:22,180
how do we know that that's happening

623
00:34:22,180 --> 00:34:25,060
or what are we not seeing all of the things

624
00:34:25,060 --> 00:34:26,660
that they did want, that they couldn't get,

625
00:34:26,660 --> 00:34:28,140
that they took off the table.

626
00:34:28,140 --> 00:34:30,140
And so I think thinking through

627
00:34:30,140 --> 00:34:33,320
as educated political observers,

628
00:34:33,320 --> 00:34:35,220
what are the things we're not observing?

629
00:34:35,220 --> 00:34:38,400
What are the things that people have made strategic choices

630
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:40,620
about to do, to not to do,

631
00:34:40,620 --> 00:34:42,880
and what should we infer from all of that

632
00:34:42,880 --> 00:34:44,220
about our political system?

633
00:34:45,920 --> 00:34:49,200
Well, what should we infer, Nicholas?

634
00:34:50,200 --> 00:34:52,800
I don't entirely know, Hannah.

635
00:34:52,800 --> 00:34:57,800
We are in a very, very polarized moment in US history.

636
00:34:57,800 --> 00:35:01,480
And I think we can say that in such polarization,

637
00:35:01,480 --> 00:35:05,680
people are looking to do something.

638
00:35:05,680 --> 00:35:09,600
Anything, anything that results in a change.

639
00:35:09,600 --> 00:35:12,400
And again, inter parties blossom and gain power

640
00:35:12,400 --> 00:35:15,640
in moments where people feel powerless.

641
00:35:15,640 --> 00:35:19,760
People feel like they're forced to wear a red or a blue suit.

642
00:35:19,760 --> 00:35:21,520
So I've been teaching for a decade

643
00:35:21,520 --> 00:35:22,920
at the University of Chicago.

644
00:35:22,920 --> 00:35:26,680
And every time I teach a class on American politics,

645
00:35:26,680 --> 00:35:29,840
And there are students, usually a minority,

646
00:35:29,840 --> 00:35:34,400
who are very interested in third parties.

647
00:35:34,400 --> 00:35:36,720
They come across the ideological spectrum,

648
00:35:36,720 --> 00:35:38,800
and so we get people on both the right and the left,

649
00:35:38,800 --> 00:35:41,360
who are very interested in the possibility

650
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:44,320
that we could break out of our existing party system.

651
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:47,760
And I feel like my sad duty in part

652
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:50,720
is to explain why that's really difficult.

653
00:35:50,720 --> 00:35:53,360
But I also think it equips people

654
00:35:53,360 --> 00:35:58,200
to think more creatively about ways to challenge the existing party system

655
00:35:58,200 --> 00:36:02,000
within the confines or constraints that we have.

656
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:05,240
And so I think the DSA is actually a really interesting example of saying,

657
00:36:05,440 --> 00:36:09,360
well, maybe we're not going to run the same race as the two major parties.

658
00:36:09,360 --> 00:36:10,720
And maybe that's not the goal.

659
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:15,680
And we can work both outside and within the system to promote the policies

660
00:36:15,680 --> 00:36:16,520
that we care about.

661
00:36:17,440 --> 00:36:21,160
You know, Nick, change is exciting to people and it is far more likely to

662
00:36:21,160 --> 00:36:26,520
happen in a midterm election. Because during a presidential election, the parties tend to

663
00:36:27,240 --> 00:36:32,200
come together to rally behind the ideals of the person running for the big chair, you know?

664
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:40,760
Midterms are where the parties themselves can be affected by, for example, a DSA, a tea party,

665
00:36:41,400 --> 00:36:48,280
a bull moose, whatever. And so I think there are students who are really intrigued by the possibility

666
00:36:48,280 --> 00:36:53,240
even if they themselves disagree with what they understand the DSA to represent.

667
00:36:53,240 --> 00:37:01,280
They see these third party movements or intra-party movements as reasons to think that our politics

668
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:08,320
is more flexible than they've been taught and that there is real opportunity to change

669
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:11,040
the status quo, which I think is really important.

670
00:37:11,040 --> 00:37:16,400
One reason to study American politics is to understand the environment you're operating

671
00:37:16,400 --> 00:37:18,920
in so that you can get the change that you want.

672
00:37:18,920 --> 00:37:25,440
And it may not be by the path that you wanted, but having some real substantive knowledge

673
00:37:25,440 --> 00:37:29,600
of how this system has worked makes it possible for you to figure out how you might want it

674
00:37:29,600 --> 00:37:30,760
to work differently in the future.

675
